## Now — The Personalized Dashboard Feature

This is a meaningfully different kind of feature than anything else you've added to DPAH so far, so let me think through it properly before handing you a plan.

### What This Actually Is, Architecturally

Looking at what you've described, the legacy system does something categorically different from your analytics workflow: instead of a chat interface that generates a fresh answer per question, this is a **persisted grid of saved widgets** — each widget is a saved prompt that gets re-executed and re-rendered whenever the user opens their dashboard. The user builds up a personal collection of "here are the 5 things I check every morning" and the system remembers and re-runs them.

This means it's not really an extension of your analytics chat — it's a **new layer on top of it**. The generation engine (prompt → SQL/RAG → chart or table) is the same core capability you already have in `analytics_workflow`. What's new is the **persistence and layout** concept: saved widget definitions, a grid position, re-execution on dashboard load, and CRUD for managing that saved set.

### The RBAC Question You Raised — Here's the Right Answer

You're right that this needs to follow the RBAC approach, but the shape is different from your existing per-collection RBAC. Existing analytics RBAC governs "can this user ask questions about RMS/HRMS/MRR data, and if so, whose rows can they see." That doesn't change here — a saved widget is just a saved prompt, and when it re-executes, it must go through the **exact same persona-scoped, schema-registry-governed SQL generation and validation pipeline** as a live chat query. A JUNIOR user's saved widget "show my hours this week" re-runs with their emp_id filter every single time it refreshes, exactly like a live query would. **You are not building a second security model — you're building a persistence layer on top of the one you already have**, which is the right way to think about it and keeps this from becoming a parallel system with its own bugs.

The new RBAC surface is narrower: **who can save/delete/reorder widgets on their own dashboard** — which is really just "does this user have any analytics access at all," not a new granular permission. Anyone with at least one `*_ANALYTICS` collection grant can use the personalized dashboard feature; the row-level filtering on each individual widget's data is inherited automatically from whatever collection/persona that widget's underlying query targets.

### What I'd Build — Concretely

**New MariaDB table** (mirroring the legacy `dp_agenticai_user_dashboard` structure, adapted to your schema conventions):

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dpah_user_dashboard_widgets (
    widget_id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    emp_id VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    collection_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,   -- e.g. "RMS_ANALYTICS" — which schema/persona context this widget runs under
    prompt_text TEXT NOT NULL,               -- the saved natural language query
    display_title VARCHAR(255),              -- optional user-friendly label, else derive from prompt
    grid_position INT NOT NULL,              -- ordering/layout slot
    output_type_hint VARCHAR(20),            -- "chart" or "table" — cached from last successful run, not authoritative
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    INDEX idx_emp_id (emp_id)
) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4;
```

**New API endpoints** (small, CRUD-shaped, no new orchestration logic needed):

```
GET    /api/v1/me/dashboard/widgets           — list current user's saved widgets, ordered by grid_position
POST   /api/v1/me/dashboard/widgets           — save a new widget {collection_name, prompt_text, display_title}
DELETE /api/v1/me/dashboard/widgets/{id}      — remove a widget (must belong to requesting user — check emp_id match)
PATCH  /api/v1/me/dashboard/widgets/reorder   — bulk update grid_position values
POST   /api/v1/me/dashboard/widgets/{id}/run  — re-execute one widget's saved prompt through the analytics graph, return fresh result
```

The `/run` endpoint is the important one architecturally — it should **not** reimplement any query logic. It loads the saved `prompt_text` and `collection_name`, constructs the exact same `AnalyticsState` your chat flow already builds, and invokes `get_analytics_graph()` exactly as `chat_controller.py` does today. This guarantees the RBAC/schema-registry/persona-filter pipeline runs identically whether the query came from live chat or from a saved widget re-running. If you ever fix a bug or improve the SQL generator prompt, both paths benefit automatically — no logic duplication, no drift between "live analytics" and "saved dashboard analytics" behaving differently over time.

**Dashboard load behavior:** when the personalized dashboard page opens, call `GET /widgets` to get the saved list, then either fire all `/run` calls in parallel (fastest, but N simultaneous LLM calls) or fire them sequentially with individual loading states per widget card (slower, gentler on your LLM gateway — given your gateway's throughput concerns from earlier in this build, I'd lean sequential-with-per-card-loading, reusing the status-message pattern from Conversation 1 above, adapted to say things like "Refreshing widget 2 of 5...").

**Adding a widget from chat:** the most natural UX, and the one that best matches your legacy system's spirit, is a small "📌 Save to Dashboard" action attached to any chart/table result already showing in the analytics chat — not a separate "build a widget" form. The user asks a question in Analytics chat, likes the chart they got back, clicks save, and it becomes a persisted widget using that exact prompt and collection context. This reuses UI you already have (the chat message with a chart/table already rendered) rather than building a parallel "widget builder" interface.

### Where This Lives in the Frontend

Given you already have a dedicated Analytics sidebar page (from the earlier work), I'd add this as a **second tab or toggle within that same Analytics page**, not a wholly separate sidebar item — something like:

```
[ Chat ]  [ My Dashboard ]     ← tabs at top of the Analytics page
```

**Chat tab** = what you have now — live conversational analytics.
**My Dashboard tab** = the grid of saved widget cards, each showing its last result, with a refresh button per card and a "Save to Dashboard" affordance surfaced from the Chat tab.

This is better than a toggle-only pattern or a brand-new sidebar entry because it keeps the mental model tight: "Analytics" is one place, and within it you either ask something new (Chat) or revisit things you've saved (My Dashboard). It also means the module selector dropdown you already built (RMS/HRMS/MRR) applies naturally — widgets from different modules can coexist on one saved dashboard, since each widget independently carries its own `collection_name`.

### What I'd Explicitly Defer

The legacy system's filter/KPI persistence tables and the "3-attempt LLM code generation loop with `exec()` in a controlled scope" pattern are both more ambitious than what you need for a first version. Your `analytics_workflow` already produces charts and tables through a safer, more constrained path (schema-registry-bounded SQL, not arbitrary generated Python executed via `exec()` — which, frankly, your current architecture is meaningfully safer than the legacy one you're replicating, since raw `exec()` of LLM-generated code is a much larger attack surface than validated SQL against a whitelisted schema). Don't reintroduce the `exec()` pattern to match legacy feature-for-feature — your existing pipeline is the better design; just point saved widgets at it.

